# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Personal verification harnesses
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are not product tooling. They mint a JWT and talk to the local dev
# database or drive Playwright against a running dev server, with one
# developer's user id, tenant id, workspace id and mailbox hardcoded at the top
# of the file. Twenty-odd of them appeared while generating one report; each is
# a single-run probe that would be rewritten next time rather than reused.
# Committing them publishes those identifiers, and 13 of them carry a real
# personal email address.
scripts/capture_*.py
scripts/seed_*.py
scripts/inspect_*.py
scripts/diagnose_*.py
scripts/test_*.py
scripts/check_rls_counts.py
scripts/generate_all_report_figures.py
scripts/build_full_report_docx.py
scripts/update_app_statuses.py
apps/web/capture_*.mjs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Report artefacts (generated, not source)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The project report and its figures are build output of the scripts above plus
# a browser pass. They are regenerated on demand and carry no information the
# repository does not already have. The .docx form of the report was already
# ignored by name; the markdown and the figures were not.
VAELOOM_*.md
VAELOOM_*.docx
docs/report-assets/
original_report_assets/

# Assistant-tool run output. Editor plugins (the Qoder "better-harness" bundle is
# the one that produced this) write each run to downloads/<tool>/<date>/<run>/,
# including a tmp-render/ copy of the same payload. It is a run log: regenerated
# per invocation, and its findings.json embeds absolute paths from the machine that
# ran it. Anchored at the repo root because the directory name is the tool's
# default and it is not created by any build or test step in this repo.
/downloads/



# Isolated production build output (see apps/web/scripts/build-isolated.cjs)
apps/web/.next-build/
# Numbered/temporary Next build copies (e.g. apps/web/.next-build2/ — 800+ files
# that otherwise flood `git status` / VS Code counts; --untracked-files=normal
# shows 1 dir entry but explorers expand to 600+ files)
apps/web/.next-build2/
.next-build2/
apps/web/.next-*/
.next-*/

# User-uploaded documents. These are tenant content, not source: they can carry
# PII and, for the documents pipeline, deliberately hostile HTML/SVG. Committing
# them would both leak customer data and reintroduce the stored-XSS payload the
# upload validator rejects. Storage belongs in S3/MinIO, not the repo.
apps/web/public/upload/
apps/web/public/uploads/

# Python coverage HTML report (the .coverage data file is ignored above)
apps/api/htmlcov/

# gstack tooling state. It ships its own .gitignore containing `*`, which only
# works because that file is already tracked-adjacent to the checkout; listing it
# here keeps the intent visible in one place and covers a fresh clone where the
# nested file has not been created yet.
.gstack/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ad-hoc browser verification artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One-off screenshots written by throwaway verification scripts (manual login
# probes, full-page capture sweeps). They are evidence of a single run, not
# source, and they are regenerated on every attempt.
#
# Deliberately NOT a blanket *.png rule: 76 PNGs are legitimately tracked, the
# Playwright visual baselines under e2e/*-snapshots/ being the important set.
# A broad rule would silently exclude new baselines from review, which is the
# opposite of what a visual-regression gate is for.
apps/web/*_test.png
apps/web/*-actual.png
apps/web/*-diff.png
apps/web/*-expected.png

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain stores that can land inside the checkout
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# None of these are produced by a normal `pnpm dev` / `pnpm test` run here, so
# they are narrow safety nets rather than observed output. Each is listed only
# because the repo already depends on the mechanism that could produce it.

# Browser binaries. apps/api/Dockerfile sets PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=/ms-playwright,
# so pointing that variable at the checkout (common in bind-mounted CI containers)
# drops several hundred MB of Chromium/Firefox/WebKit inside the working tree.
ms-playwright/

# pnpm's content-addressable store. The repo's .npmrc does not pin store-dir, so
# today it lives in the user's home; `pnpm config set store-dir` puts the entire
# store - every version of every dependency - in the project instead.
pnpm-store/

# Editable-install metadata. This is a Python monorepo with many packages, and
# `pip install -e` writes *.egg-info next to each one. None are tracked today.
*.egg-info/
